menu "WiFi Dap core (CMSIS-DAP command engine)"

    config BIRDIEBOT_DAP_STATS
        bool "Enable DAP core profiling counters + Vendor9 stats command"
        default n
        help
            Debug-only instrumentation for OpenOCD/elaphureLink throughput work.

            Counts per-command processing time and the gap between consecutive
            host commands (transport round trip), the command-type histogram,
            0x7E/0x7F batch statistics, SWD transfer count and
            DAP_TransferBlock word counts.  A CMSIS-DAP Vendor9 command
            (0x89, sub-command 1 = snapshot, 0 = reset) returns the counters so
            a host-side raw-DAP client (elaphureLink port 3240) can read them
            remotely without serial console access.

            Production builds keep this disabled: no counters, no time reads,
            no Vendor9 handler.

    config BIRDIEBOT_SWD_ULTRA_FAST
        bool "ESP32-S3 dedicated-GPIO SWD engine (all SWD rates, 100 kHz-20 MHz)"
        default y
        depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3
        help
            Enable the M5 Xtensa dedicated-GPIO SWD engine
            (components/DAP/cmsis-dap/source/SWD_Fast_GPIO.c) and make it the
            ONLY SWD backend on the ESP32-S3: SWD_Transfer at every adapter
            speed, SWJ_Sequence (line reset / JTAG-to-SWD / dormant wake-up)
            and SWD_Sequence (capture) all run on it.  The legacy GPIO
            normal/fast and 40 MHz SPI SWD backends are compiled out for this
            target; if the engine cannot run (wrong core, pins not routed,
            CPU < 150 MHz) the SWD entry points return DAP errors instead of
            silently falling back.

            Rate coverage: 100 kHz .. 20 MHz on the default 160 MHz CPU build,
            up to 24 MHz on a 240 MHz CPU build.  The engine picks a level
            that is never faster than the host request (the mapping keeps the
            error within 0 .. -25 %); high rates use fully unrolled assembly,
            low rates use cycle-counter paced loops.

            On hardware: 12/16 MHz were stable on an STM32H723, 20 MHz was
            marginal on that target/wiring - OpenOCD `adapter speed 16000` is
            the recommended setting there (docs/swd-23mhz-engine.md §6).

            Default is y per the M4/M5 user requests.  The engine is not yet
            validated on every target/wiring combination: set this to n to
            restore the legacy GPIO/SPI backend selection exactly, and read
            docs/swd-23mhz-engine.md for the HIL checklist and rollback.
            Only affects ESP32-S3 SWD; JTAG, ESP32, C3 and C5 are unchanged.

    config BIRDIEBOT_SWD_ULTRA_MIN_CLOCK_HZ
        int "DEPRECATED: minimum DAP_SWJ_Clock request (Hz, 0 = engine for all rates)"
        default 0
        depends on BIRDIEBOT_SWD_ULTRA_FAST
        range 0 50000000
        help
            Deprecated in M5 and kept only for build compatibility with the M4
            DAP_SWJ_Clock wiring: the engine now covers every SWD rate, so the
            default 0 means "no minimum - the engine is used for all adapter
            speeds".  It will be removed once DAP_SWJ_Clock no longer
            references it (see docs/swd-23mhz-engine.md §4).

            A non-zero value would make DAP_SWJ_Clock fall through to the
            legacy backend selection below that request; do not set it on a
            build with M5 wiring.

    config BIRDIEBOT_SWD_ULTRA_OEN_STAY_ON
        bool "Keep SWDIO output enabled during SWD turnaround (A-fork workaround)"
        default n
        depends on BIRDIEBOT_SWD_ULTRA_FAST
        help
            SWD requires the host to release SWDIO during the turnaround and
            the ACK/data phases driven by the target.  The ultra engine does
            that by default (same approach as XRDAP-ESP, including the
            GPIO_ENABLE read-back and settle delay).

            The current GPIO fast path on this board keeps the SWDIO pad
            output enabled all the time (the A 2025 fork workaround, see
            DAP_config.h PIN_SWDIO_OUT_ENABLE/DISABLE on ESP32-S3), and that
            is the configuration known to pass OpenOCD bring-up on this board.
            Enable this option to make the ultra engine use the same
            "always driven" behaviour for the HIL A/B comparison if the
            protocol-correct turnaround fails on a particular target/wiring.

            Leave it disabled unless HIL debugging says otherwise.

endmenu
